  m_Name: PetrolEngineData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Petrol Engine
  engineDescription: A Petrol Engine is an internal combustion engine that converts
    the chemical energy of petrol into mechanical energy through spark-ignition combustion.
    It generally operates on the Otto cycle, consisting of intake, compression, combustion
    and expansion, and exhaust processes. Its major components include the cylinder,
    piston, connecting rod, crankshaft, spark plug, fuel injector or carburetor,
    intake and exhaust valves, camshaft, cooling system, and lubrication system.
    During operation, an air-fuel mixture enters the cylinder and is compressed by
    the piston. Near the end of the compression stroke, the spark plug ignites the
    mixture, producing high-pressure gases that force the piston downward during
    the power stroke. The crankshaft converts the reciprocating motion of the piston
    into rotary motion. Petrol engines are widely used in cars, motorcycles, small
    generators, and light-duty vehicles. From an engineering perspective, compression
    ratio, ignition timing, air-fuel ratio, combustion efficiency, thermal efficiency,
    cooling, lubrication, emissions, and fuel injection are important factors influencing
    engine performance.
  engineCategory: Engineering
